The Security Council,

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of Syria, and to the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,

Recalling its resolutions 2042 (2012), 2043 (2012), 2118 (2013), 2139 (2014), 2165 (2014), 2175 (2014), 2191 (2014), 2209 (2015), 2235 (2015), 2249 (2015), 2254 (2015), 2258 (2015), 2268 (2016), 2286 (2016), 2332 (2016), 2336 (2016), 2393 (2017) and 2401 (2018), and its presidential statements on the situation in Syria,

Recalling the Convention on the Prohibition of the Development, Production, Stockpiling and Use of Chemical Weapons and on their Destruction, and the decisions of the Executive Council of the Organisation for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons (OPCW),

Expressing grave concern at the reports of a chemical weapons attack in Douma, Syria, on 7 April 2018, which reportedly caused numerous casualties, including civilians,

Expressing outrage at the continued use of chemical weapons in the Syrian Arab Republic, and recalling that the use of chemical weapons constitutes a serious violation of international law and a threat to international peace and security,

Reaffirming that those responsible for any use of chemical weapons must be held accountable, and stressing the need for a thorough, independent and impartial investigation in accordance with the Chemical Weapons Convention,

Reaffirming its resolution 2401 (2018), which demanded that all parties cease hostilities without delay and allow safe, unimpeded and sustained humanitarian access throughout Syria, and expressing grave alarm at the continued lack of humanitarian access and the dire situation of civilians in Eastern Ghouta, Idlib Governorate, Rukban and other areas,

Underlining the need for all parties to respect their obligations under international humanitarian law and international human rights law, including the protection of civilians, medical personnel and humanitarian workers, and to demilitarize medical facilities and schools,

Reaffirming its support for the political process in line with resolution 2254 (2015), and stressing that there can be no military solution to the Syrian conflict,

Determining that the devastating humanitarian situation in Syria and the use of chemical weapons continue to constitute a threat to peace and security in the region,

1. Condemns in the strongest terms the reported chemical weapons attack in Douma, Syria, on 7 April 2018, and deplores the loss of life;

2. Reaffirms that the use of chemical weapons in the Syrian Arab Republic constitutes a serious violation of international law and a threat to international peace and security, and stresses that those responsible for the use of chemical weapons must be held accountable;

3. Demands that the Syrian authorities and all other parties to the conflict cooperate fully with the OPCW and provide immediate, safe, unimpeded and unfettered access to the OPCW fact-finding mission to Douma and to all other sites, and provide all necessary information to enable a thorough and independent investigation;

4. Requests the OPCW to report to the Security Council on the findings of its investigation as soon as possible, and requests the Secretary-General to keep the Council informed of the situation;

5. Reaffirms resolution 2401 (2018) and demands its full and immediate implementation by all parties, including the cessation of hostilities, the lifting of all sieges, and the safe, unimpeded and sustained delivery of humanitarian aid and services and medical evacuations to all persons in need throughout Syria, including in Eastern Ghouta, Idlib Governorate, Rukban and other hard-to-reach and besieged areas;

6. Reaffirms that the cessation of hostilities shall not apply to military operations against the Islamic State in Iraq and the Levant (ISIL, also known as Da’esh), Al Qaeda and the Al Nusra Front (ANF), and all other individuals, groups, undertakings and entities associated with Al Qaeda or ISIL, and other terrorist groups as designated by the Security Council;

7. Demands that all parties allow immediate, safe and unhindered access for humanitarian personnel and medical evacuations, and ensure the protection of civilians, medical personnel and humanitarian workers, and refrain from attacks against civilians and civilian objects, including medical facilities and schools;

8. Calls upon all parties to respect international humanitarian law and international human rights law, and to facilitate the delivery of humanitarian assistance to all areas in need;

9. Reaffirms its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of Syria, and to the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations;

10. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council within 15 days on the implementation of this resolution and on compliance by all parties, and decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

